From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LIVI appliance
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:00:00 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] usb: musb: deliver a pending Rx packet instead of flushing it

musb_ep_restart() flushes the Rx FIFO whenever a request is queued to a
non-busy OUT endpoint (commit 3fc137386, "usb: musb: Fix hardware lockup
on first Rx endpoint request").  The flush was chosen over calling
rxstate() there because rxstate() invokes the request's completion
callback from inside usb_ep_queue(), and a callback that re-enables
interrupts mid-giveback (u_ether's rx_complete() calling netif_rx()) can
then re-enter the driver and leave RXPKTRDY set with no request queued.

That trade-off silently drops a packet the host has already had ACKed.
USB bulk-only mass storage is exactly such a protocol: the next CBW is
pipelined onto the wire ~30us after the CSW, so it can be ACKed into the
Rx FIFO before f_mass_storage's thread wakes up and re-queues its CBW
request.  The restart then flushes that CBW; fsg waits forever for a
command that will never be resent (BOT has no retransmission), the host
waits forever for the data phase, and the whole LUN is dead until a USB
reset - a 30 second DID_TIME_OUT and a frozen file read on the unit.

Deliver a packet that is already in the FIFO to the request being queued
instead.  The cleanup the flush provided is not lost where it matters:
f_mass_storage flushes its endpooints on every exception, and endpoint
disable/enable resets the FIFO, so genuinely stale packets are still
dropped by those paths; the one case this changes is the packet the host
will not send again.

The completion callback runs from usb_ep_queue() again, as it did before
3fc137386.  That is the layering violation the udc API warns about, and
it is why the flush was introduced; the lockup it can cause needs a
completion callback that enables interrupts mid-giveback, which none of
the functions this board runs (f_mass_storage, FunctionFS) has.  Without
the flush the alternative for those functions is losing their transfer.

Signed-off-by: LIVI appliance
---
 drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c | 19 ++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c
@@ -1170,9 +1170,22 @@
 		txstate(musb, req);
 	} else {
 		csr = musb_readw(epio, MUSB_RXCSR);
-		csr |= MUSB_RXCSR_FLUSHFIFO | MUSB_RXCSR_P_WZC_BITS;
-		musb_writew(epio, MUSB_RXCSR, csr);
-		musb_writew(epio, MUSB_RXCSR, csr);
+		if (csr & MUSB_RXCSR_RXPKTRDY) {
+			/*
+			 * The host's packet is already in the Rx FIFO:
+			 * it arrived before this request was queued
+			 * (mass storage pipelines its next CBW onto
+			 * the wire ~30us after the CSW).  It has been
+			 * ACKed and the host will not resend it, so
+			 * hand it to the request the way the Rx
+			 * interrupt would instead of flushing it away.
+			 */
+			rxstate(musb, req);
+		} else {
+			csr |= MUSB_RXCSR_FLUSHFIFO | MUSB_RXCSR_P_WZC_BITS;
+			musb_writew(epio, MUSB_RXCSR, csr);
+			musb_writew(epio, MUSB_RXCSR, csr);
+		}
 	}
 }
 
